legal campaign contributions days before the election", "Pingree holds a roughly 2-1 fundraising and cash-on-hand advantage", "Prediction markets price Democratic odds near 95% and most raters call the race Solid D"], "rationale": "Every recent poll puts Pingree up eight to thirteen points, prediction markets price her near ninety-five percent, and raters overwhelmingly call the race Solid D. Charles's late ethics fine and Pingree's two-to-one money edge reinforce a clear Democratic advantage in this open-seat contest.", "watch": "A last-minute escalation of the ethics case against Charles or a surge by independent Rick Bennett under ranked-choice voting could shift the count, though not enough to threaten the lead.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Janet Mills (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Hannah Pingree enters the final stretch of Maine's open governor's race with a clear lead. Polls from more than a dozen surveyors since early September consistently put her eight to thirteen points ahead of Republican Bobby Charles, with the most recent mid-to-late October surveys showing a ten-to-eleven-point margin. Prediction markets price her chances around ninety-five percent, and most race raters call the contest solidly or likely Democratic.\n\nCharles faces a late complication: the state Ethics Commission fined him more than twenty-two thousand dollars over illegal campaign contributions, with a possible investigation still pending. Pingree has also out-raised him roughly two to one and picked up endorsements from the firefighters union and Wabanaki chiefs. An independent, Rick Bennett, is on the ballot but polling well behind. The seat is open because Janet Mills is term-limited, removing any incumbency effect.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9757, "margin": 10.9}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9716, "margin": 10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9705, "margin": 10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9685, "margin": 10.5